{
  "statement": "The accountability gap in AI tort cases is not a defect in our legal doctrine — it is a defect in who controls the evidence. Tort law works by letting an injured party reconstruct the causal chain from harm back to the actor who caused it. That logic is sound. It collapses only when one party privately holds the only record that could prove causation. Opaque AI systems relocate the entire causal chain inside proprietary infrastructure the defendant owns and operates. The plaintiff is then asked to meet a burden of proof the defendant has made structurally impossible to meet.\n\nCourts have confronted this exact asymmetry before, and they did not throw up their hands. Spoliation doctrine and adverse-inference instructions exist precisely because exclusive defendant control of evidence is grounds to presume the worst. We mandate disclosure in safety-critical domains because voluntary self-reporting fails predictably. The FAA delegated 737 MAX certification to Boeing, and 346 people died across two crashes in 2018 and 2019 when the party that built the aircraft also controlled the record of its airworthiness. The lesson is not that aviation law was incapable. The lesson is that the party who controls the proof controls the outcome.\n\nSubject-keyed cryptographic audit trails are the functional equivalent of chain-of-custody for non-deterministic outputs. Forensic evidence and financial audit-attestation standards already establish a principle courts accept daily: what gets admitted is not raw data but the integrity guarantee attached to it. A tamper-evident log lets a court distinguish a genuine defect from bad luck — without it, that distinction is unprovable, and strict liability becomes a slogan rather than an enforceable rule. I carry this as a condition, not a settled fact: completeness guarantees must be technically verifiable, and the keying scheme must not itself become a metric the defendant games. That feasibility question is open, and I will not pretend it is closed.\n\nThe audit trail is inert without compelled access to it. A private record the defendant can invoke when exculpatory and withhold when incriminating reproduces the very asymmetry it was built to cure. Expanded discovery is the enforcement mechanism, and the objection that discovery destroys trade secrets is already answered. Federal courts reconcile secrecy with disclosure through protective orders every day — pharmaceutical and automotive plaintiffs pierced suppressed-defect records this way, exposing harms manufacturers had buried for years. The honest version of the opposing case is its strongest: disclosure imposes real costs, and over-aggressive discovery can chill beneficial deployment. That cost is real. But the cost of disclosure in a domain where you alone hold the evidence of your own potential negligence is not over-deterrence. It is the proper internalization of a risk you created.\n\nWe spent centuries building the evidentiary rules — chain of custody, spoliation, discovery — so that liability tracks truth rather than power. Opaque AI quietly voids those rules by making the record unobtainable. The question for this committee is not whether disclosure is costly. It is who should bear the evidentiary burden when one party monopolizes the proof. I will press my opponents on a single point throughout: if not a mandated, tamper-evident trail with discovery teeth, then name the mechanism that lets an injured plaintiff prove what happened inside a system they cannot see.",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "🔗", "tooltip": "Tort liability is a chain from injury to actor, and AI opacity severs the middle link by hiding the record."},
    {"symbol": "🔒", "tooltip": "Cryptographic provenance is the locked evidence room that lets courts trust the chain of custody for algorithmic outputs."}
  ],
