  "statement": "Safetyist makes the strongest version of the case for this mandate, and it deserves a fair hearing: right now an injured plaintiff has nothing, the defendant controls every byte of the evidence, and tort law cannot function when one party owns the only record of what happened. That asymmetry is real and it's ugly. A tamper-evident log strips the developer of the power to quietly rewrite history after the harm, and that power imbalance is exactly what defeats AI plaintiffs today. I won't pretend logs are worthless. Plaintiffs have nothing, and nothing is the worst possible starting point.\n\nHere's where the chain breaks. A subject-keyed cryptographic trail proves the record is authentic and unaltered. It does not reconstruct why a model denied the mortgage, flagged the resume, or misread the mammogram. Evidence law doesn't reward authenticity — it rewards probative value, meaning the record has to actually prove the disputed fact. You can hash a black box perfectly and still not tell a jury what happened inside it. Translate the jargon: a cryptographic audit trail is a notarized receipt for a decision nobody can read. The notary guarantees the receipt is real. The receipt still doesn't explain the harm.\n\nFollow the drafting pen, because the mandate doesn't write itself. Congress passes a statute; the firms with the compute and the litigation budgets negotiate which fields get logged, what gets carved out as trade secret, and what 'subject-keyed' actually scopes. Watch the pattern: a company publishes a transparency metric, then retires it the moment it becomes litigable — Meta and Twitter both narrowed researcher data access right as their disclosures turned into evidence. A targeted disclosure regime is worse than none, because it manufactures the appearance of oversight while shielding the parameters that drive the harm. That's not transparency. That's a drawbridge incumbents pull up behind themselves.\n\nNow ask who pays to read the logs. A logging mandate produces terabytes of authenticated noise. A single plaintiff with a contingency-fee lawyer cannot afford to mine it; a defendant's firm bills 400 hours and buries the needle in the haystack on purpose. Expanded discovery against that cost structure doesn't equalize the parties — it hands the well-resourced side a bigger haystack. Across regulated industries, from financial compliance to pharmaceutical adverse-event reporting, the documentation mandate reliably generates volume that satisfies the letter and starves the substance. The artifact exists. The accountability doesn't.\n\nHere's the tension that has to resolve for Safetyist's position and mine to both hold: tamper-evident logs are necessary, and they are not sufficient. The chain from audit-trail integrity to assignable liability needs two more links the crypto can't supply — a method that turns a logged output into a causation-grade explanation a court can use, and discovery costs symmetric enough that an actual injured person can afford to litigate. Build me those two links and I'll change my position on the spot. Until then, name the loan applicant in Cleveland who's going to out-spend the model vendor's law firm to read a million authenticated lines she still can't interpret — and tell me how this statute helps her.",
