  "statement": "Safetyist makes the strongest version of the institutional case: the accountability gap is not about doctrine, it's about who holds the evidence. When a lending model denies a loan or a diagnostic tool misreads a scan, the plaintiff cannot see inside the box, and the developer controls every log that could prove fault. That asymmetry is real. A subject-keyed cryptographic trail — one tamper-evident record per affected person — would hand the plaintiff a chain of custody they can authenticate in court. I will not pretend that's a trivial concern. It's the genuine insight in their position, and any honest accelerationist has to answer it.\n\nHere's where the evidence points the other way: tort law has assigned liability under deep causal opacity for fifty years without demanding the manufacturer hand over a cryptographic confession. The DES litigation of the 1980s produced market-share liability — plaintiffs who couldn't identify which manufacturer made the drug that injured them still recovered, apportioned by market share. Pharmaceutical, aircraft, and medical-device plaintiffs win against black-box systems constantly. They don't get source code. They get burden-shifting, res ipsa loquitur, and expert inference. The gap Safetyist names is evidentiary access friction, not a doctrinal void — and that distinction decides who pays for the fix.\n\nNow scale the cost of the proposed fix. A permanent, Congress-mandated logging architecture is a regressive transparency tax. Sarbanes-Oxley raised audit costs by roughly $1.5 million per public company and measurably pushed small firms toward going private and away from IPOs. The largest AI labs will absorb a subject-keyed logging mandate as a rounding error. The open-source contributor and the eight-person startup will not. You do not close an accountability gap by handing the three biggest incumbents the only key to the gate. That's the distributional question Safetyist's framing skips, and it's not negotiable for me: who bears the cost matters as much as whether the trail exists.\n\nThe market is already producing the trail without an 18-month federal mandate gestating. Insurers writing AI errors-and-omissions coverage now demand auditability as a condition of the policy — no log, no coverage, no deployment. That signal is more honest than a federal checkbox, because the insurer prices its own money against the actual risk, and a performative compliance log doesn't fool an actuary. I'll bound that claim: if someone shows insurers are not in fact demanding litigation-grade auditability, my position weakens, and the bounded fallback is a narrow evidentiary-preservation rule — not a national cryptographic regime. My argument rests on one assumption I'll name plainly: that insurance and reputation pressure reach deep enough to produce trails a court can use.\n\nSo here is the tension that decides this debate. For Safetyist's mandate to be necessary, you have to believe existing burden-shifting doctrine cannot reach AI defendants — and that the cost of a permanent logging architecture falls evenly. Both can't be true. If tort law already shifts the burden to the party controlling the evidence, the mandate is redundant friction. If it doesn't, the fix is a targeted discovery-sanction and spoliation rule that a court applies case by case — reversible, cheap, and blind to whether you're OpenAI or a kid with a GitHub repo. Every month that 18-month mandate sits in markup is a month AI diagnostics get priced out of reach for everyone but the labs that can afford the compliance build. Who gave the drafters the right to make that trade for the patients waiting on the other side?",
