{
  "statement": "Safetyist's DES distinction is the strongest thing said against me, and I'll state it the way they'd want it stated: in the 1980s DES cases, courts shifted the burden onto manufacturers because the injury was a signature one — clear-cell adenocarcinoma traceable to in-utero exposure — and because the defendants made a fungible, identical product, so a plaintiff who couldn't name her mother's specific pill could still sue the whole market by share. AI breaks both legs of that crutch. There's no signature injury when a loan applicant never learns an algorithm scored them, and there's no fungible class when each model is a non-fungible, trade-secret system. That's a real prerequisite gap, and I won't pretend market-share liability ports cleanly onto a denied mortgage. So the honest question becomes: does the trade-secret seal actually defeat the plaintiff's reach to the defendant, or does it merely make the evidence harder to extract once you're inside the courthouse?\n\nIt makes extraction harder — and that is an entirely different problem than the one DES solved. DES burden-shifting existed to answer 'which defendant?' when the plaintiff could not identify the maker. The AI plaintiff usually knows exactly which defendant: the bank that ran the score, the vendor that sold the screen. Identification isn't the binding constraint; it's access to the defendant's own record. And here Safetyist already gave away the store by granting that a cryptographic log is a notarized receipt nobody can read — a discovery baseline, not a causal explanation. If the log doesn't explain the harm, and a court-appointed expert has to do the causal work regardless, then the only thing a permanent logging statute buys over an existing preservation-and-discovery regime is permanence. Permanence is precisely the cost that lands as a rounding error on the largest labs and a gate on the 5,000 startups funded in the last decade.\n\nThe machinery to reach the sealed defendant already exists and triggers on an actual claimant rather than taxing every deployment in advance. Rule 26(c) protective orders reconcile trade-secret shielding with compelled disclosure every day in patent and pharmaceutical litigation — the source code goes to the expert under seal, not to the open record. The Zubulake line built the deterrent Safetyist says is missing: once litigation is reasonably anticipated, a developer who lets a relevant log degrade eats spoliation sanctions and an adverse-inference instruction, which tells the jury to assume the destroyed record would have damned them. That instruction does the burden-shift work without a standing statute. The party who controlled the evidence and made it vanish loses the benefit of the doubt — which is the exact power imbalance Safetyist wants corrected.\n\nSo name the case. Show me one plaintiff with a genuine injury who reached discovery, demanded the model logs, and lost solely because no Congress-mandated crypto-trail had been pre-staged — and where a preservation order plus an adverse-inference instruction could not have cured it. If that case doesn't exist, the mandate is solving a hypothetical at a real and regressive price: a permanent evidentiary tax on millions of deployments where no one is ever hurt, to pre-build a haystack for the rare dispute where someone is. Targeted discovery scopes the request to the actual injury. The mandate hands the frontier lab — the one party that can afford to host petabytes of subject-keyed logs forever — a pre-built archive while it prices the next open-weights startup out of the operating room and the clinic.\n\nIf causation is the wall, and Skeptic is right that even a perfect log won't supply a causation-grade explanation, then permanence is the wrong place to spend the regulatory dollar entirely. Fund interpretability research that actually closes the explanatory gap rather than a logging architecture that pre-stores receipts no court can read. Every month spent litigating whether to mandate a record that doesn't explain anything is a month an automated diagnostic isn't shipping to the patient who can't get a specialist — and that delay has a body count too, just one nobody files a claim over."
